 "understand": [
  {
   "p": "Look at these sentences first, before any rule:"
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "هَذَا كِتَابٌ.",
     "en": "This is a book."
    },
    {
     "ar": "هَذَا قَلَمٌ.",
     "en": "This is a pen."
    },
    {
     "ar": "هَذَا بَيْتٌ.",
     "en": "This is a house."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "p": "Arabic has <strong>no word for “is”</strong> in sentences like these. You just put the two words side by side: <span class='ar'>هَذَا</span> + <span class='ar'>كِتَابٌ</span> = “this (is) a book”. And there's no separate word for “a”: the ending <em>-un</em> (tanwīn) already means “a”."
  },
  {
   "p": "Now questions:"
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "مَا هَذَا؟",
     "en": "What is this?"
    },
    {
     "ar": "هَذَا مِفْتَاحٌ.",
     "en": "This is a key."
    },
    {
     "ar": "مَا هَذَا؟ هَذَا سَرِيرٌ.",
     "en": "What is this? This is a bed."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "rule": "<span class='ar'>هَذَا</span> points to something <strong>near</strong> and masculine. <span class='ar'>مَا</span> asks “what?” about things (not people) and goes at the start of the sentence. The Arabic question mark faces the other way: <span class='ar'>؟</span>"
  },
  {
   "tip": "The nouns in this lesson are all grammatically masculine. Feminine nouns get their own word for “this” in lesson 8."
  }
 ],
